WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.590
Foundations. The line.

2
00:00:09.458 --> 00:00:11.628
How does a change reach a running service?

3
00:00:13.234 --> 00:00:15.084
Start with the partition fork.

4
00:00:15.474 --> 00:00:17.404
It owns a short list of paths:

5
00:00:17.474 --> 00:00:22.684
the Azure provider, its Azure tests,
and a few files that describe the fork.

6
00:00:23.394 --> 00:00:25.724
Its workflows come from a template.

7
00:00:26.034 --> 00:00:30.444
Everything else is the community's code,
regenerated from upstream every day.

8
00:00:30.994 --> 00:00:33.404
The fork's own paths stay put.

9
00:00:35.654 --> 00:00:39.184
A change reaches a running
service through six stations.

10
00:00:39.334 --> 00:00:41.744
Upstream, where the community works.

11
00:00:42.214 --> 00:00:46.624
Sync, which brings upstream's
changes into the fork every day.

12
00:00:47.014 --> 00:00:50.384
Cascade, which builds them
with the fork's own code.

13
00:00:50.774 --> 00:00:53.584
Main, the fork's protected branch.

14
00:00:53.974 --> 00:00:56.544
And release, which gives it a version.

15
00:00:57.014 --> 00:01:01.424
Beside them is the lane, where
a new image runs in a stack.

16
00:01:01.894 --> 00:01:05.094
Every service fork comes
from that one template,

17
00:01:05.094 --> 00:01:07.974
so every fork has the same six.

18
00:01:07.974 --> 00:01:10.624
A fork's own change joins at main.

19
00:01:12.906 --> 00:01:17.146
Every eligible commit gets an image,
and a turn in a running stack,

20
00:01:17.146 --> 00:01:19.236
before any release exists.

21
00:01:19.466 --> 00:01:22.436
In the lane, the new
image runs in the stack,

22
00:01:22.586 --> 00:01:24.756
and the service's own tests check it.

23
00:01:25.226 --> 00:01:28.596
So a release only tags an
image that's already there.

24
00:01:28.906 --> 00:01:30.676
Nothing is rebuilt.

25
00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:36.216
Follow one upstream change along the line.

26
00:01:36.366 --> 00:01:38.776
It lands in the community's repository.

27
00:01:39.086 --> 00:01:43.016
At midnight UTC, sync
proposes it to the fork.

28
00:01:43.246 --> 00:01:45.726
Cascade builds it with the Azure provider,

29
00:01:45.726 --> 00:01:47.896
and its image takes a turn in the lane.

30
00:01:48.126 --> 00:01:52.216
It reaches main, and the image
built from main takes its turn too.

31
00:01:52.686 --> 00:01:55.656
Main's image is the one
a stack runs by default.

32
00:01:55.966 --> 00:01:59.896
A release, when one comes, tags
an image that's already there.

33
00:02:02.162 --> 00:02:03.532
So now you can say:

34
00:02:03.762 --> 00:02:09.282
a fork keeps a few paths of its own,
every fork has the same six stations,

35
00:02:09.282 --> 00:02:13.372
and a change's image runs in a
stack before a release ever tags it.

36
00:02:15.858 --> 00:02:19.148
Next: who does what,
and which path is mine?

